<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huang Chao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/884">884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huang Chao was a failed examination candidate and salt smuggler who led the largest rebellion of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. Rising in 875, his army marched through much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in 879 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 881, where he proclaimed the state of Qi. Driven out in 883 by Tang forces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, he was killed in 884. His former officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huang-chao.jpg" alt="Huang Chao" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%BB%83%E5%B7%A2%EF%BC%88%E6%AE%98%E5%94%90%E4%BA%94%E4%BB%A3%E5%8F%B2%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Huang Chao came from Yuanqu, in the present-day Cao County of Heze, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, and his birth date is not known. His family had been salt privateers for generations, since the state had monopolised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>, and the Huang family became wealthy from smuggling. He was said to be capable in swordsmanship, riding and archery and somewhat capable in writing and rhetoric. He took in desperate men with his wealth, and they served under him.</p><p>He sat the imperial examinations repeatedly without passing them and then resolved to rebel against Tang rule. The dynasty had passed its golden age. The power of the provincial military governors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiedushi">jiedushi</a>, had grown, and impoverished farmers, tax-burdened landowners, merchants and large salt-smuggling operations formed the base of the rebellions of the mid-ninth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Huang Chao joined the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xianzhi">Wang Xianzhi</a> against the Tang in the mid-870s. After he split with Wang, his army turned south and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. In 881 his troops captured the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and forced Emperor Xizong to flee. Huang moved into the palace and declared himself emperor of a new state of Qi, making his wife, Lady Cao, empress and Shang Rang among others chancellors.</p><p>He at first tried to take over the Tang imperial mandate. Officials of the fourth rank or lower were kept in office if they registered their submission, and those of the third rank or above were removed. Tang officials who would not submit were executed en masse. Several Tang generals submitted to him, but each eventually declared loyalty to the Tang again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In spring 883 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> general, and other Tang generals defeated a Qi force of 150,000 men commanded by Shang Rang. In summer 883 Li Keyong entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and Huang abandoned the city and fled east. Defeated again, including by his former subordinates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> and Shang Rang, who had gone over to the Tang, he withdrew into the Langhu Valley. On 13 July 884 his nephew Lin Yan was reported to have killed him, though the reports are inconclusive and an alternative account in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a> says that he killed himself. Some have suggested that the heads brought to the Tang were a decoy, and legends of the following centuries said he became a Buddhist monk.</p><p>Huang Chao also wrote poetry. One of his lines, &quot;The capital's full of golden armored soldiers&quot;, later gave its name to the Chinese title of the 2006 film Curse of the Golden Flower.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huang Chao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last Tang emperors were puppets of competing warlords, and the dynasty's formal end came in 907 when Zhu Wen, a former lieutenant of <b>Huang Chao</b> who had defected to the Tang and risen to dominate the court, deposed the last emperor and founded the Later Liang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 873 Tuoba Sigong seized Youzhou; for helping drive <b>Huang Chao</b> from Chang'an he received in 881 the four prefectures of the Dingnan Jiedushi and in 883 the imperial surname Li and the title Duke of Xia, a frontier command his house held through the Five Dynasties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Huang Chao</b>'s rebel army quickly took Luoyang, the eastern capital of the Tang dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Li Keyong, chief of the Shatuo Turks, helped the Tang drive <b>Huang Chao</b> out of Chang'an in 883 and was rewarded with the governorship of Hedong, centred on Taiyuan.</blockquote><a href="/state/jin-five-dynasties">Jin (Five Dynasties)</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhu Wen, a lieutenant of <b>Huang Chao</b>, defected to the Tang in 882 and was made governor at Bianzhou (Kaifeng).</blockquote><a href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Huang Chao</b>, a salt smuggler who had failed the civil service examinations, joined Wang Xianzhi's rebellion in 875 and led it after Wang's death in 878.</blockquote><a href="/state/qi-huang-chao">Qi (Huang Chao)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Heze, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.23,115.43&amp;year=874" aria-label="Heze on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7472 890 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Huang Chao"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7922 1160h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7922 1160h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.23,115.43&amp;year=874">What was here in 874 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
